fix(core,loop): scope the merge signal per overlay and ignore fossil streaks (#4044)

An independent cold review of this PR found two defects in the back-pressure
signal, with a reproduction test. Both were real.

Cross-overlay bleed: `read_merge_signal` counted `PullRequest.live()` and the
aged `SweepSkipStreak` rows GLOBALLY while feeding `_issue_intake_scanner_for`,
which is per-overlay. A stall in one overlay braked intake in another. Invisible
on a single-overlay box; a silent cross-tenant brake the moment a second exists.
The reader now takes `overlay=` and filters both queries; the caller passes
`backend.name`.

Fossil streaks: `SweepSkipStreak.resolve` only fires on a live `pr_sweep.*`
signal for that exact (slug, pr_id), so a PR that merges or closes outside the
sweep leaves its row behind forever. Counting rows independently of the live set
let those fossils outnumber real PRs — the reviewer's repro shows 3 healthy PRs
and 5 settled-PR rows reading as a stall. The old `min(stuck, open_prs)` clamp
made it worse: it guaranteed the ratio hit 1.0 whenever fossils outnumbered live
PRs. Streaks are now intersected against live PR keys, so a row only counts when
it names a PR that still exists.

Fails OPEN by construction: a stuck PR whose streak row was pruned reads as
healthy and the factory keeps working. That matches this module's existing
doctrine that an unreadable probe never brakes — the cost of a false brake
(the factory stops while everything is fine) is worse than the cost of a late
one (some wasted work).

Also merges origin/main, keeping both this branch's MergeSignal and main's
WRITE_CONCURRENCY_PER_CORE = 0.5 from #4069, and both test classes.

Verified: the reviewer's repro was RED against the previous code and is GREEN
here; 89 passed across the repro, the governor tests and the intake wiring.
